<onlyinclude>'''Information metabolism''' is a historical theory of mental life associated with the Polish psychiatrist Antoni Kępiński (1918–1972). It compares the exchange and organization of information between a person and their surroundings with the exchange and transformation of substances and energy in living organisms. It is a model of experience and adaptation, not a demonstrated chemical or neurological pathway.

== What the comparison means ==
People continually encounter situations that matter differently to them. A sound can be a warning to one person and a harmless background noise to another. Perception, past experience, bodily condition and emotion influence what the person attends to and how they respond. Kępiński used the analogy of metabolism to bring such exchanges into a broader account of psychiatric experience.

The comparison does not mean that ideas are nutrients or that the amount of meaningful information can be measured directly in units of energy. [[Metabolism/Simple|Biological metabolism]] consists of chemical reactions; [[Information theory/Simple|information theory]] measures uncertainty in a specified mathematical model.

== History and interpretation ==
Kępiński's psychiatric work included the experiences of survivors of Auschwitz. Later researchers have interpreted his theory in different ways: some emphasize its biological analogy, while others emphasize emotional and interpersonal experience. The theory also influenced [[Socionics/Simple|socionics]], a later personality typology; this is a separate system, not a necessary implication of Kępiński's work.

== Evidence and limitations ==
Historical importance does not establish that a model accurately predicts cognition or mental illness. Information metabolism is not a validated diagnostic test or a substitute for clinical assessment. Modern research on emotions, perception and decision making can investigate narrower questions without accepting the whole historical theory.

